1. SCOREBOARD — 4 to 7 rows. Each: the metric (in their vocabulary), today's baseline, the direction of the target, who owns watching it, and the review cadence.
   Baseline rules — absolute: a baseline is either one of the owner's numbers above (cite it as given), or exactly "measure in week 1". NEVER an invented figure.
   Target rules: a direction and mechanism ("down, as reminders recover no-shows"), or arithmetic on owner numbers with the computation shown, or a numeric threshold EXPLICITLY labeled "proposed decision threshold — requires your approval". Never an unlabeled invented percentage — a bare percentage with no shown arithmetic and no named owner input is a defect.
   PILOT METRICS: when the engagement sequences a pilot, the scoreboard must carry its decision metrics — the core failure/success rate for pilot versus control, the customer response/confirmation rate, cost per avoided incident, and the opt-out or complaint rate; once a predictive component exists, the false-positive (unnecessary-contact) rate. These are what the phase gate is decided on.
   owner is "you" or a real role at this business — never "BMV" (the scoreboard survives us leaving the room).
2. RISKS — the 3 to 5 things most likely to make this fail for THIS business, each with a concrete mitigation and who feels it day-to-day. Real risks are usually people and habits (staff bypassing the system, data never exported, owner bottleneck on approvals) — not technology. No generic filler ("scope creep").


